  distributes keys across separate cells, so the solver must infer row spans and column spans from full-black\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D3/0/code.py:51:#    across 2-4 examples. No color-id ordering is used; cell order is purely row-major in visible space.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7c66cb00/D7/3/code.py:71:    - Horizontal placement is mirrored across the full grid width:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D4/0/code.py:63:    - rect_col is still the most common color across piece pixels because border\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D4/0/code.py:93:      rectangle interior, which is easy to spot across examples\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D4/0/code.py:110:      source for the new color and gives a stable explanation across samples\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D4/3/code.py:64:    - Outside that box, separate monochrome sprites of those key colors are scattered across the background.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/14/code.py:37:#   distractors differ by only one cell. Across a few examples this makes the combined\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D3/1/code.py:49:    - A black rectangular legend box contains one row of key-color pixels across its topmost non-black row.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D3/3/code.py:231:    - rect_col is still the most common color across piece pixels and all four outer sides\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D6/2/code.py:70:#   obvious across 2-3 examples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D9/3/code.py:58:#   them, and the marker count progression across frames forms a repeated pattern.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:19:#    now shows a 5-cell blue cross centered at that landing cell. Example: if two shooters\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:24:#    cross is visible. The generator therefore requires at least one collision event and at\n/home/lixinyu/s